Nmap Network Scanning Lab

Overview

This project documents my beginner-level exploration of network scanning and reconnaissance using Nmap in a controlled virtual lab environment.

Objective

The objective was to:

* Discover hosts within a lab network
* Identify open, closed, and filtered ports
* Identify services running on discovered ports
* Understand the difference between network scanning and enumeration

Lab Environment

* Kali Linux
* Metasploitable
* VirtualBox
* Nmap

Tools Used

Nmap — Network discovery and port scanning

VirtualBox — Virtualized lab environment

Kali Linux — Security testing environment

Scanning

I used Nmap to scan selected ports on the target machine.

Example:

nmap -p 21,22,23,80 10.0.2.15

The scan was used to examine common services including FTP, SSH, Telnet, and HTTP.

What I Observed

The scan returned different port states, including filtered and closed ports.

I learned that:

* An open port indicates that an application is actively accepting connections.
* A closed port indicates that the port is reachable but no application is listening.
* A filtered port means that a firewall or other network filtering mechanism is preventing Nmap from determining whether the port is open.

Challenges

One of the challenges I encountered was configuring the virtual machines so that Kali Linux and Metasploitable could communicate correctly on the same virtual network.

This helped me understand the importance of correct network configuration before performing security testing.

Key Takeaways

This lab helped me understand the practical process of network reconnaissance and gave me hands-on experience interpreting Nmap scan results.

I also learned that scanning results need to be interpreted in the context of the network configuration rather than assuming that every result represents a security vulnerability.

